Do not confuse this graph with the displacement-time graph of 7.1.4. There the height of the graph was the position \(x\), and its slope gave the velocity. Here the height is the velocity \(v\) itself. So in a velocity-time graph:
the height at any instant tells how fast (and in which direction) the particle moves at that instant;
the slope tells how quickly the velocity changes, which is the acceleration;
the area under the graph tells how far the particle has moved, which is the displacement.
The rest of this page explains why the slope and the area have these meanings.
